10:33:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Hail forms high up inside strong thunderstorms, where temperatures are well below freezing even if it’s hot at the ground. Here’s the basic process: - Warm, humid summer air rises rapidly in a thunderstorm. - High in the cloud, the air can be far below 0°C / 32°F. - Tiny water droplets remain liquid even below freezing (“supercooled” water). - When those droplets hit an ice particle, they freeze onto it. - Powerful updrafts can carry the growing ice stone up and down through the storm multiple times, adding layers of ice. - Eventually the hailstone becomes too heavy for the updraft to hold up and falls. As it falls through the warm air below the storm, it starts to melt. Large hailstones can reach the ground before fully melting, especially because they fall quickly and the air directly beneath a thunderstorm may be cooled by rain and descending air. If the ice melts completely before reaching the ground, it arrives as rain instead.
